 ## Contract
 
+### Connection intake is grouped by mechanism
+
+The existing three picker views mean account sign-in, API access and terminal access. Classify
+from access.type, not the payment plan: a Coding Plan requiring an API key belongs to API access.
+The template's plan/product identity, catalog, quota reader and credential owner do not change.
+Native CLI entries create native-cli connections, never a generic subscription-login form.
+Their setup reads that CLI's supported account/configuration; the current Claude/Codex adapters
+still support their declared subscription route, not arbitrary API-configured native sessions.
+
+Fleet's default scheduling remains Pi Agent Core under the Host. A shipped Claude/Codex executable
+is an optional native executor, not Fleet's core or a signed-in account. Terminal cards show
+detected installation/version only after a successful version probe; shipped-but-broken binaries
+are unavailable. Detection failure is not evidence that no executable is installed. Keep the
+original controls and layout, use the existing native connection operation, and preserve saved
+connections, drafts, permissions and all enabled/retired route decisions. No data migration occurs.
+
+Verify the complete picker → exact selected template → mechanism-specific setup → original
+create callback, including API-key plans, direct account login and native CLI. Renderer cases
+must reject the old subscription CTA and API fields on native setup, keep back navigation usable,
+and exercise English/Chinese and light/dark. This proves local routing, not vendor entitlement.
+
 The existing Provider config owns access type, API format, endpoint and credential. A
 bound model captures these facts. Its request-auth overlay may refresh credentials;
 it cannot select a different endpoint or protocol. No additional credential store,
